    <h2>The Two Fencing Markets and Their Rules</h2>

    <p>In the subdivisions, the first question is not what fence you want — it's what the HOA allows. Covenants in neighborhoods like Turtle Pond Estates and Booth Meadows routinely spell out height, material, and style, and they mean it. Skip that step and here is the result: they build a beautiful fence, a neighbor or the HOA flags it, and now they are paying to tear down and rebuild to the rulebook they ignored. Out on the rural lots there's freedom but scale — long runs of field fence, gates wide enough for equipment, and terrain that a suburban crew isn't set up for. A fence company that knows Hartselle asks where you live before it talks materials, because that answers half the questions.</p>

    <h2>The Challenge Underneath Every Fence: The Clay</h2>

    <p>Whatever the rules, the ground is the same problem everywhere in Morgan County. The clay holds water and moves with the seasons, and that movement is what leans and heaves a poorly set fence. The fix is depth and drainage: posts set 24 to 36 inches down into stable soil, spaced 6 to 8 feet apart, in concrete footings crowned to shed water away from the post instead of holding it against the wood. The practitioner detail that separates a lasting fence from a leaning one: a wood post dropped into a concrete cup that traps water rots at grade within a few years, so the footing has to be shaped to drain. Set shallow to save labor and the fence tells on the shortcut by its second summer.</p>

    <h2>Choosing the Material — and What Lasts Here</h2>

    <p>Wood is the default for subdivision privacy fence — warm, affordable up front, and usually HOA-approved — but it needs staining every few years to survive Alabama humidity, and that upkeep is the cost people forget. Vinyl and aluminum cost more to install and almost nothing to maintain, which can make them cheaper over ten years despite the higher sticker. Chain link is the workhorse for back lots and rural runs. The honest admission a good fence company makes: the cheapest fence to install is rarely the cheapest fence to own, and sometimes your HOA decides the material regardless of what you'd pick. Fencing often lands in the same season as other yard work, so it pairs naturally with a <a href="/locations/hartselle/concrete/">concrete</a> crew pouring a new patio and a <a href="/locations/hartselle/landscaper/">landscaper</a> regrading the beds along the new line.</p>

    <h2 id="pricing-summary">What Fencing Costs in Hartselle</h2>

    <p>Real ranges, installed:</p>
    <ul class="trades-list-block">
      <li><strong>Wood privacy fence</strong> — about $25 to $40 per linear foot.</li>
      <li><strong>Chain link</strong> — roughly $12 to $20 per linear foot.</li>
      <li><strong>Vinyl or aluminum</strong> — about $30 to $55 per linear foot, higher up front, lower to maintain.</li>
      <li><strong>Gates, corners, and rocky or sloped runs</strong> — priced on top, because they are where the real labor is.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>A typical quarter-acre backyard, roughly 150 feet of 6-foot wood privacy fence, runs near $4,000 to $6,000. Post depth and footing quality are where a cheap bid quietly saves money you'll pay back in repairs.</p>

    <h2>Timing Your Fence Project</h2>

    <p>Spring and fall are the busy, ideal fencing seasons — the ground works well and crews book up, so start early if you want a spring fence. If you're in an HOA subdivision, begin the approval process before you schedule anyone; that paperwork is the long pole, not the build. New homeowners in the subdivisions are smart to fence before the kids and dogs arrive rather than after, when it becomes an emergency.</p>

    <h2>How a Fence Fits the Bigger Yard Project</h2>

    <p>Fencing rarely happens alone — it's usually one line item in a homeowner making the whole yard usable at once, alongside grading, hardscape, and drainage. The <a href="/locations/hartselle/">Hartselle contractor overview</a> lays out how those outdoor trades connect, and the fence company that understands the HOA-and-clay realities of this specific market is the one that gets the neighborhood's repeat work.</p>

    <h2>Common Fencing Questions in Hartselle</h2>

    <p><strong>Do I need HOA approval?</strong></p>
    <p>In the subdivisions, almost always — height, material, and style are often dictated, and building without approval can force a tear-down. Rural lots usually have no such rules.</p>

    <p><strong>Why do my fence posts lean?</strong></p>
    <p>Shallow posts in Morgan County clay that heaves. Posts need 24 to 36 inches of depth in drained concrete footings to stay straight.</p>

    <p><strong>What does it cost?</strong></p>
    <p>Wood privacy $25 to $40 a foot; chain link $12 to $20; vinyl or aluminum $30 to $55. A typical backyard runs $4,000 to $6,000.</p>

